/// Screwdriver bits and screwdrivers whose tips are a fastener's recess read as
/// a solid, so a bit fits its screw exactly.

// Driver Library (driver)
//
// The tools that turn fasteners: screwdriver bits and screwdrivers. A bit is a
// TIP on a SHANK; a screwdriver is a tip on a BLADE in a HANDLE.
//
// A TIP IS THE RECESS READ FROM THE OTHER SIDE. A screw's recess is a void from
// mechanical/drill, and the tip is that same field as a solid, so a bit seated
// in the recess it is for meets it flank to flank and passes through it
// nowhere — the rule thread.male and thread.female follow, for the same reason:
// one definition read from both sides is the only way a fit between two parts
// means anything.
//
// A CROSS RECESS NUMBER IS ONE FORM, which is what makes that work for every
// screw a number goes in rather than one: the recesses are one shape sunk to
// different depths.
//
// A CROSS TIP IS ISO 4757'S GAUGE, which the standard says is identical
// with the point of the screwdriver: its own Table 2, a hair smaller than the
// recess everywhere, built by the same ribs as the recess (drill.phillipsGauge).
// It is not the recess read backwards, so it does not touch at one depth by
// construction; ISO 7045 bounds where it touches instead, between its minimum
// and maximum penetration.
//
// Conventions: millimetres; axis +Z; z = 0 is the END OF THE TIP, the gauge
// point, with the shank running up +Z. To seat a Phillips bit, put its end at
// the minimum penetration:
//
//   bit = driver.bit(driver.phillipsTip(2.0), driver.insertShank(driver.fluteLength(2.0), 0.7));
//   seated = remap(bit, [p.x, p.y, p.z - fastener.panPhillipsSeat(4.0)]);
//
// sits in fastener.panPhillips(4.0, l) a few hundredths off its ribs and
// nowhere inside it. A Pozidriv bit's end goes at fastener.panPozidrivSeat.
//
// Tips are bounded above by their own working length and every shank starts
// there, so a bit is one min of two solids. They are VALUES, like fastener's
// heads and shanks: no closures.
//
// Doc convention follows builtin/base.gsl: `///` lines are public docs.

// =========================================================
// Tool Sizes
// =========================================================
//
// These are TOOL standards, keyed by the tool's own size rather than by a
// thread diameter, so they live here rather than in mechanical/catalog.
//
// VERIFY BEFORE TRUSTING A VALUE: the ISO 8764 and ISO 1173 figures were read
// from summaries of those standards, not the standards; the handle sizes are
// one maker's and ILLUSTRATIVE — no standard sizes a handle.
//
// REFERENCES:
//   ISO 8764-1:2004 https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/37562/1b12535b14e946d38e404288c41ce8e7/ISO-8764-1-2004.pdf
//   ISO 8764-2:2004 https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/37556/dd6b8591fa2c47929844a3d8db938c9b/ISO-8764-2-2004.pdf
//   ISO 1173:2001   https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/23588/ab32510c11f74bfbb198fcd63d2b9a04/ISO-1173-2001.pdf
//   ISO 2380-1:2004 https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/35869/8172b254f966470ab774a4c0a99231a2/ISO-2380-1-2004.pdf
//   ISO 2380-2:2004 https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/35870/1d7d096dde084f0bbd110af0d7bfc184/ISO-2380-2-2004.pdf
//   bit lengths, which ISO 1173 does not set — Wera 851/1 Z, 851/4 Z, Wiha micro bit:
//                   https://www.wera.de/en/tools/851-1-z-bits
//                   https://www.wera.de/en/tools/851-4-z-bits
//                   https://www.wiha.com/int/en/tools/bits/wiha-micro-bit/1620/micro-bit-28-mm
//   handles, illustrative — Wera 350 PH, Wiha SoftFinish:
//                   https://www.wera.de/en/tools/350-ph-screwdriver-for-phillips-screws
//                   https://digicat.wiha.com/tools/screwdrivers/mechanic-s-screwdrivers/softfinish/phillips/screwdriver-softfinish/27756
//   Torx bit sizes  https://www.wihatools.com/pages/torx-sizes

// Cross recess screwdrivers, ISO 8764-1 and -2, by recess number. Columns:
//   [recess number, blade diameter, fluted length of the tip (min),
//    blade length (series B), a size of ISO 7045 screw the number goes in]
// The last column is only there for phillipsGauge and pozidrivGauge: any size
// of the number gives the same cone radius on the floor.
crossTool = [[0.0,  3.0, 2.78,  60.0, 2.0],
             [1.0,  4.5, 2.78,  75.0, 3.0],
             [2.0,  6.0, 4.37, 100.0, 4.0],
             [3.0,  8.0, 6.74, 150.0, 6.0],
             [4.0, 10.0, 8.34, 200.0, 8.0]];

// A screwdriver HANDLE by recess number: [recess number, diameter, length].
// ILLUSTRATIVE, and PH4 has none.
handleTable = [[0.0, 23.0, 104.0],
               [1.0, 30.0, 111.0],
               [2.0, 36.0, 118.0],
               [3.0, 41.0, 124.0]];

/// ISO 8764-1 blade diameter of a cross recess screwdriver of this recess
/// number: 6 for a PH2 or a PZ2.
bladeDia(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    crossTool));
}

/// The fluted length of a cross tip of this recess number — how far up the tip
/// the recess form runs before the tool is solid.
fluteLength(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    crossTool));
}

/// ISO 8764-2 series B blade length for this recess number: 100 for a PH2.
bladeLength(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[3] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    crossTool));
}

// crossScrew: an ISO 7045 size the recess number goes in.
crossScrew(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[4] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    crossTool));
}

/// An illustrative screwdriver handle diameter for this recess number.
handleDia(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[1] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    handleTable));
}

/// An illustrative screwdriver handle length for this recess number.
handleLength(recessNo) {
  k = recessNo;
  return reduce((acc, v) { return acc + v; },
                map((r) { return r[2] * catalog.onRow(k, r[0], 0.0001); },
                    handleTable));
}

// =========================================================
// Tips
// =========================================================
//
// Each tip is a solid from z = 0, its end, up to its working length, and open
// nowhere: the recess voids are unbounded above, so a tip bounds itself.

/// The FLOOR RADIUS of a Phillips recess number in ISO 7045: where its 26 degree
/// 30 cone, m wide at the head, comes down to at the maximum penetration — m/2
/// less the penetration times tan 26.5, 1.00 for a PH2. Every size a number goes
/// in gives the same one, which is what makes the number one recess.
///
/// A CHECK ON THE CATALOG, NOT A DIMENSION OF THE TIP. phillipsTip is ISO 4757's
/// gauge and does not read this; it is kept because it is the one number that
/// ties ISO 7045's per-size m and penetration back to the recess number.
phillipsGauge(recessNo) {
  size = crossScrew(recessNo);
  return catalog.panPhillipsDia(size) / 2.0 - catalog.panPhillipsDepth(size) * 0.49858161;
}

/// The floor radius of a Pozidriv recess number in ISO 7045, the same way — a
/// catalog check, not what pozidrivTip is built from.
pozidrivGauge(recessNo) {
  size = crossScrew(recessNo);
  return catalog.panPozidrivDia(size) / 2.0 - catalog.panPozidrivDepth(size) * 0.49858161;
}

/// A PHILLIPS (ISO 4757 type H) tip of this recess number, up the fluted
/// length: ISO 4757's own gauge, whose point the standard says "is identical
/// with the point of the respective screwdriver" — drill.phillipsGauge at
/// Table 2's numbers, its flat end at z = 0. ISO 4757:1983 section 2.2,
/// https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/10742/d878fa0371e041b39de75de4d4426928/ISO-4757-1983.pdf
phillipsTip(recessNo) {
  return drill.phillipsGauge(0.0, fluteLength(recessNo),
                             catalog.phillipsGaugeCore(recessNo), catalog.phillipsGaugeFacet(recessNo),
                             catalog.phillipsGaugePoint(recessNo), catalog.phillipsGaugeFlat(recessNo),
                             catalog.phillipsGaugeRidge(recessNo), catalog.phillipsGaugeFlank(recessNo),
                             catalog.phillipsGaugeRoot(recessNo));
}

/// A POZIDRIV (ISO 4757 type Z) tip of this recess number, up the fluted
/// length: ISO 4757's type Z gauge (Table 4, read from IS 7478,
/// https://archive.org/details/gov.in.is.7478.2011) — drill.pozidrivGauge,
/// its point at z = 0.
pozidrivTip(recessNo) {
  point = catalog.pozidrivGaugePoint(recessNo);
  len = fluteLength(recessNo);
  knee = catalog.pozidrivGaugeKnee(recessNo);
  form = drill.pozidrivGauge(point, len,
                             catalog.pozidrivGaugeCore(recessNo), catalog.pozidrivGaugeWing(recessNo),
                             knee, point,
                             catalog.pozidrivLean(recessNo), catalog.pozidrivValley(recessNo));
  // The gauge's 26 degree 30 cone is widest at zTop = len: a safe over-bound.
  rim = knee / 2.0 + (len - point) * 0.49858161;
  form.box = [[-rim, -rim, 0.0], [rim, rim, len]];
  return form;
}

/// A TORX (ISO 10664) tip of this T number, `length` long: the hexalobular
/// section, straight-sided, at the recess's own dimensions.
torxTip(tNumber, length) {
  major = catalog.torxMajor(tNumber);
  form = max(drill.torxAt(length, catalog.torxMajor(tNumber), catalog.torxMinor(tNumber), length),
             p.z - length);
  form.box = [[-major / 2.0, -major / 2.0, 0.0], [major / 2.0, major / 2.0, length]];
  return form;
}

/// A HEX tip `acrossFlats` wide and `length` long — the key for a hex socket.
hexTip(acrossFlats, length) {
  corner = acrossFlats * 0.57735027;
  form = max(drill.hexSocketAt(length, acrossFlats, length), p.z - length);
  form.box = [[-acrossFlats / 2.0, -corner, 0.0], [acrossFlats / 2.0, corner, length]];
  return form;
}

/// A ROBERTSON (square) tip of this size number, `length` long, ending in the
/// four-sided point of the 1909 patent: drill.robertsonAt read as a solid.
robertsonTip(sizeNo, length) {
  across = catalog.robertsonAcross(sizeNo);
  form = max(drill.robertsonAt(length, catalog.robertsonAcross(sizeNo), length),
             p.z - length);
  form.box = [[-across / 2.0, -across / 2.0, 0.0], [across / 2.0, across / 2.0, length]];
  return form;
}

/// A SLOTTED tip, ISO 2380 form A: a blade `thickness` by `width` at its end,
/// `length` long. Its faces open at the standard's 10-degree wedge for the
/// first 1.5, which is the part that sits in the slot, and then at 30 degrees
/// (included, like the first), which is how the blade gets back to the bar it
/// was ground from in the length of a bit rather than of a crowbar. Each face
/// leans half the included angle from the axis: 5 degrees, then 15.
slotTip(thickness, width, length) {
  edge = abs(p.y) * 0.99619470 - p.z * 0.08715574 - thickness / 2.0 * 0.99619470;
  ramp = abs(p.y) * 0.96592583 - (p.z - 1.5) * 0.25881905 - thickness / 2.0 * 0.96592583;
  // Both wedges open upward, so the blade is thickest at its top.
  flare = thickness / 2.0 + max(length * 0.08748866, (length - 1.5) * 0.26794919);
  form = max(min(edge, ramp), abs(p.x) - width / 2.0, neg(p.z), p.z - length);
  form.box = [[-width / 2.0, -flare, 0.0], [width / 2.0, flare, length]];
  return form;
}

// =========================================================
// Shanks
// =========================================================
//
// Every shank starts at `zLow`, where its tip stops, with a 45-degree NECK
// coming out of radius `neckR` — the tip's core — so the tool closes over the
// ends of the flutes the way a ground bit does.

// neck: the 45-degree cone a shank grows out of, `neckR` at zLow.
neck(zLow, neckR) {
  return ((length(p.xy) - neckR) - (p.z - zLow)) * 0.70710678;
}

// driveEnd: the corner chamfer on the drive end of a hex bar at zTop.
driveEnd(acrossFlats, zTop) {
  return ((length(p.xy) - 0.52 * acrossFlats) + (p.z - zTop)) * 0.70710678;
}

/// A 1/4-INCH INSERT BIT's shank, ISO 1173 form C 6.3: hex 6.32 across flats,
/// 25 long overall, with the V-groove on its corners 8.2 from the drive end
/// that a magnetic or spring holder catches.
insertShank(zLow, neckR) {
  groove = (3.35 + abs(p.z - 16.8) - length(p.xy)) * 0.70710678;
  form = max(drill.hexXY(6.32), zLow - p.z, p.z - 25.0, neck(zLow, neckR),
             driveEnd(6.32, 25.0), neg(groove));
  // The hex bar, flats on x and corners on y.
  form.box = [[-3.16, -3.6488537, zLow], [3.16, 3.6488537, 25.0]];
  return form;
}

/// A 1/4-INCH POWER BIT's shank, ISO 1173 form E 6.3 — the SELF-LOCKING one: a
/// round groove, root diameter 4.7 at 9.5 from the drive end, that the ball of
/// a quick-release chuck drops into. 50 long, hex over the last 25, with a
/// slimmer round torsion zone between it and the tip (4.4 across,
/// ILLUSTRATIVE).
powerShank(zLow, neckR) {
  torsion = max(length(p.xy) - 2.2, zLow - p.z, p.z - 25.5, neck(zLow, neckR));
  hexPart = max(drill.hexXY(6.32), p.z - 50.0, driveEnd(6.32, 50.0),
                ((length(p.xy) - 2.2) - (p.z - 25.0)) * 0.70710678);
  ball = length([length(p.xy) - 4.75, p.z - 40.5]) - 2.4;
  form = max(min(torsion, hexPart), neg(ball));
  // The hex starts 22.8 up at the latest, where its lead-in cone leaves the
  // torsion zone.
  form.box = [[-3.16, -3.6488537, min(zLow, 22.8)], [3.16, 3.6488537, 50.0]];
  return form;
}

/// A 4 mm PRECISION BIT's shank, ISO 1173 form C 4: hex 3.94 across flats,
/// 28 long overall.
microShank(zLow, neckR) {
  form = max(drill.hexXY(3.94), zLow - p.z, p.z - 28.0, neck(zLow, neckR),
             driveEnd(3.94, 28.0));
  form.box = [[-1.97, -2.2747601, zLow], [1.97, 2.2747601, 28.0]];
  return form;
}

/// A bit: `tip` and `shank` together.
bit(tip, shank) {
  return min(tip, shank);
}

// =========================================================
// Screwdrivers
// =========================================================
//
// TWO FUNCTIONS, BLADE AND HANDLE, because they are two materials — a steel
// blade in a plastic handle — and a material is a fact about a named object.
// They share a datum, so they cannot drift apart: the blade's tip end at z = 0,
// the handle from the top of the blade's exposed length up.

/// A screwdriver BLADE: round, `bladeDia` across, from its tip's working length
/// `zLow` (necked from `neckR`) up `bladeLen` of exposed blade and on another
/// 25 into the handle. Min it with a tip.
screwdriverBlade(zLow, neckR, bladeDia, bladeLen) {
  form = max(length(p.xy) - bladeDia / 2.0, zLow - p.z, p.z - (zLow + bladeLen + 25.0),
             neck(zLow, neckR));
  form.box = [[-bladeDia / 2.0, -bladeDia / 2.0, zLow],
              [bladeDia / 2.0, bladeDia / 2.0, zLow + bladeLen + 25.0]];
  return form;
}

/// A screwdriver HANDLE `dia` across and `len` long, starting at `zBase`: a
/// ferrule taper at the front, a rounded butt, and six shallow grip flutes.
/// ILLUSTRATIVE — the shape of a handle is a maker's, not a standard's.
screwdriverHandle(zBase, dia, len) {
  r = dia / 2.0;
  top = zBase + len;
  // The butt is a hemisphere on the end of the cylinder: the sphere bounds
  // only what is above its own centre, or the whole handle shrinks into it.
  body = max(length(p.xy) - r, zBase - p.z,
             ((length(p.xy) - 0.55 * r) - (p.z - zBase) * 0.8) * 0.78086881,
             min(p.z - (top - r), length([p.x, p.y, p.z - (top - r)]) - r));
  flute = max(length([p.x - 1.1 * r, p.y]) - 0.28 * r,
              (zBase + 0.3 * len) - p.z, p.z - (top - 0.25 * len));
  form = max(body, neg(remap(flute, map.patternPolarXY(6))));
  form.box = [[-r, -r, zBase], [r, r, top]];
  return form;
}
